{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"classes-objects","title":"Classes & Objects","group":"Classes & Objects","order":26,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A class bundles data (members) with the functions that operate on it (member functions), and controls access with <code>public</code>, <code>private</code> and <code>protected</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Class</strong> — a user-defined type. An <strong>object</strong> is an instance of it. The class's <em>invariant</em> (e.g. “balance is never negative”) is protected by keeping data <code>private</code> and changing it only through member functions.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Defining a class</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nclass BankAccount {\npublic:                                          // the interface\n    BankAccount(std::string owner, double balance)\n        : owner_(std::move(owner)), balance_(balance) {}\n\n    void deposit(double amount) {\n        if (amount &lt;= 0) throw std::invalid_argument(\"amount must be positive\");\n        balance_ += amount;\n    }\n    bool withdraw(double amount) {\n        if (amount &gt; balance_) return false;     // invariant: balance_ &gt;= 0\n        balance_ -= amount;\n        return true;\n    }\n    double balance() const { return balance_; }  // const: doesn't modify the object\n    const std::string&amp; owner() const { return owner_; }\n\nprivate:                                         // the implementation\n    std::string owner_;\n    double balance_;\n};\n\nint main() {\n    BankAccount acc(\"Ada\", 100);\n    acc.deposit(50);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; acc.withdraw(500) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; acc.withdraw(30) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; acc.owner() &lt;&lt; \": \" &lt;&lt; acc.balance() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    // acc.balance_ = 1e9;   // error: 'balance_' is a private member\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0 1\nAda: 120</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Access</th><th>Accessible from</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>public</code></td><td>Anywhere</td></tr><tr><td><code>protected</code></td><td>The class, its friends and derived classes</td></tr><tr><td><code>private</code></td><td>Only the class itself and its friends</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-compare\"></i><code>struct</code> vs <code>class</code></h3>\n<p>They are the same thing except for defaults: <code>struct</code> members and bases are <strong>public</strong> by default, <code>class</code> ones <strong>private</strong>. Convention: <code>struct</code> for plain data bundles, <code>class</code> when there's an invariant to protect.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nstruct Point {                  // aggregate: public data, no constructors needed\n    int x = 0;\n    int y = 0;\n    int manhattan() const { return (x &lt; 0 ? -x : x) + (y &lt; 0 ? -y : y); }\n};\nint main() {\n    Point p{3, -4};                        // aggregate initialisation\n    Point q{.x = 1, .y = 2};               // designated initialisers (C++20)\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; p.manhattan() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; q.y &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>7 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i><code>const</code> member functions &amp; <code>this</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nclass Counter {\n    int value_ = 0;\n    mutable int reads_ = 0;               // mutable: may change even in const functions\npublic:\n    Counter&amp; increment() { ++value_; return *this; }   // return *this to allow chaining\n    int value() const { ++reads_; return value_; }\n    int reads() const { return reads_; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Counter c;\n    c.increment().increment().increment();\n    const Counter&amp; view = c;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; view.value() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; view.value() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; view.reads() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    // view.increment();    // error: increment() is not const\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 3 2</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li><code>this</code> is a pointer to the object the member function was called on; <code>*this</code> is the object.</li><li>A <code>const</code> member function gets a <code>const T* this</code> — it can't modify members (except <code>mutable</code> ones) or call non-const members.</li><li>Only <code>const</code> member functions can be called on <code>const</code> objects and through <code>const&amp;</code> — so mark every non-mutating function <code>const</code>.</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-files\"></i>Declaring in a header, defining in a <code>.cpp</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nclass Circle {\npublic:\n    explicit Circle(double r);\n    double area() const;           // declared here (header)\nprivate:\n    double r_;\n};\n\nCircle::Circle(double r) : r_(r) {}                 // defined outside with ClassName::\ndouble Circle::area() const { return 3.14159 * r_ * r_; }\n\nint main() { std::cout &lt;&lt; Circle(2).area() &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>12.5664</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What are the four pillars of OOP and how does C++ express them?</summary><div><p><strong>Encapsulation</strong> — <code>private</code> data + public member functions. <strong>Abstraction</strong> — abstract classes / pure interfaces. <strong>Inheritance</strong> — <code>class D : public B</code>. <strong>Polymorphism</strong> — virtual functions (run time) and overloading/templates (compile time).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the size of an object?</summary><div><p>The sum of its non-static data members plus padding (and a vptr if it has virtual functions). Member functions and static members don't take space in each object. An empty class has size 1.</p></div></details></section>"}